\subsection{Reserved Words and Context-Sensitive Lexing}\label{sec:reserved-words}

\begin{definition}[Reserved Word]\label{def:reserved-word}
  A \emph{reserved word} (keyword) is a lexeme that has a fixed meaning in
  the language and cannot be used as an identifier.
\end{definition}

Some languages have \emph{context-sensitive} lexical rules where the set of
valid tokens depends on the parsing context:

\begin{example}[Context-Sensitive Lexing in C]\label{ex:c-context-sensitive}
  In C, the token \texttt{T} could be either:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item A \texttt{TYPEDEF\_NAME} (if \texttt{T} was declared as a type).
    \item An \texttt{IDENTIFIER} (otherwise).
  \end{itemize}
  The lexer must consult the symbol table to determine which interpretation
  is correct.  This is a rare example of context-sensitive lexical analysis
  in a mainstream language.
\end{example}

%% ============================================================
%% 16.3 Regular Expressions and Finite Automata
%% ============================================================
\section{Regular Expressions and Finite Automata}\label{sec:regex-automata}

The theory of regular expressions and finite automata is the mathematical
foundation of lexical analysis.  We develop it here with an emphasis on its
application to lexer construction.

\subsection{Regular Expressions}\label{sec:regular-expressions}

\begin{definition}[Regular Expression]\label{def:regex}
  A \emph{regular expression} over an alphabet $\Sigma$ is defined
  inductively:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item $\varepsilon$ is a regex; it denotes $\{\varepsilon\}$.
    \item For each $a \in \Sigma$, $a$ is a regex; it denotes $\{a\}$.
    \item $\emptyset$ is a regex; it denotes $\emptyset$.
    \item If $r$ and $s$ are regexes, then so are:
      \begin{itemize}
        \item $r \mid s$ (alternation): denotes $L(r) \cup L(s)$.
        \item $r \cdot s$ (concatenation): denotes $L(r) \cdot L(s)$.
        \item $r^*$ (Kleene star): denotes $L(r)^*$.
      \end{itemize}
  \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Regex for Lexical Patterns]\label{ex:regex-patterns}
  \begin{align*}
    \texttt{identifier} & = [a\text{--}zA\text{--}Z\_][a\text{--}zA\text{--}Z0\text{--}9\_]^* \\
    \texttt{integer} & = [0\text{--}9]^+ \\
    \texttt{float} & = [0\text{--}9]^+\texttt{.}[0\text{--}9]^+([\texttt{eE}][\texttt{+-}]?[0\text{--}9]^+)? \\
    \texttt{string} & = \texttt{"}(\texttt{\textbackslash\textbackslash} . \mid [^\texttt{"\textbackslash\textbackslash}])^*\texttt{"}
  \end{align*}
\end{example}

\subsection{Finite Automata}\label{sec:finite-automata}

\begin{definition}[NFA]\label{def:nfa}
  A \emph{nondeterministic finite automaton} (NFA) is a quintuple
  $(Q, \Sigma, \delta, q_0, F)$ where:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $Q$ is a finite set of states.
    \item $\Sigma$ is the input alphabet.
    \item $\delta : Q \times (\Sigma \cup \{\varepsilon\}) \to \mathcal{P}(Q)$
      is the transition function.
    \item $q_0 \in Q$ is the start state.
    \item $F \subseteq Q$ is the set of accepting states.
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[DFA]\label{def:dfa}
  A \emph{deterministic finite automaton} (DFA) is an NFA where:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $\delta : Q \times \Sigma \to Q$ (no $\varepsilon$-transitions,
      no nondeterminism).
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Regex to NFA (Thompson's Construction)]\label{thm:thompson}
  For every regular expression $r$, there exists an NFA $M$ such that
  $L(M) = L(r)$.  The NFA can be constructed in $O(|r|)$ time and has at
  most $2|r|$ states.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[NFA to DFA (Subset Construction)]\label{thm:subset}
  For every NFA $M$ with $n$ states, there exists a DFA $M'$ with at most
  $2^n$ states such that $L(M') = L(M)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[DFA Minimization]\label{thm:minimize}
  For every DFA $M$, there exists a unique (up to isomorphism) minimal DFA
  $M'$ such that $L(M') = L(M)$.  The minimal DFA can be constructed in
  $O(n \log n)$ time.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{From Regex to Lexer}\label{sec:regex-to-lexer}

The standard pipeline for constructing a lexer from regex specifications is:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Regex to NFA}: Thompson's construction.
  \item \textbf{NFA to DFA}: Subset construction (powerset construction).
  \item \textbf{DFA minimization}: Hopcroft's algorithm or Moore's algorithm.
  \item \textbf{Code generation}: Emit a table-driven or direct-coded lexer.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
