%% ============================================================
%% 16.4 Lexer Generators
%% ============================================================
\section{Lexer Generators}\label{sec:lexer-generators}

Lexer generators automate the construction of lexers from regex
specifications.  We survey the most important tools.

\subsection{Lex and Flex}\label{sec:lex-flex}

\begin{historical}
Lex was developed by Mike Lesk and Eric Schmidt at Bell Labs in 1975, as
part of the Unix toolchain~\cite{lesk1975lex}.  It was the first widely
used lexer generator.  Flex (Fast Lexical Analyzer Generator) by Vern Paxson
is the modern successor, producing faster scanners.
\end{historical}

A Lex/Flex specification has three sections:
\begin{verbatim}
  definitions
  %%
  rules
  %%
  user code
\end{verbatim}

\begin{example}[Flex Specification]\label{ex:flex}
  \begin{verbatim}
  %{
  #include "parser.tab.h"
  %}
  
  digit    [0-9]
  letter   [a-zA-Z_]
  
  %%
  "if"       { return IF; }
  "else"     { return ELSE; }
  "while"    { return WHILE; }
  {letter}({letter}|{digit})*  { yylval.str = strdup(yytext); return ID; }
  {digit}+   { yylval.num = atoi(yytext); return NUMBER; }
  [ \t\n]+   { /* skip whitespace */ }
  "//".*     { /* skip line comment */ }
  .          { return yytext[0]; }
  %%
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{re2c}\label{sec:re2c}

re2c is a lexer generator that produces C code optimized for speed:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Direct coding}: Generates switch-based code instead of
    table-driven code, enabling compiler optimizations.
  \item \textbf{No runtime library}: The generated code is self-contained.
  \item \textbf{Used in practice}: PHP, V8, and other performance-critical
    projects use re2c.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[re2c Specification]\label{ex:re2c}
  \begin{verbatim}
  /*!re2c
    re2c:define:YYCTYPE = char;
    re2c:yyfill:enable = 0;
    
    digit = [0-9];
    alpha = [a-zA-Z_];
    
    alpha (alpha | digit)* { return IDENT; }
    digit+                  { return NUMBER; }
    [ \t\n]+               { continue; }
    *                      { return ERROR; }
  */
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{ANTLR Lexer Mode}\label{sec:antlr-lexer}

ANTLR (ANother Tool for Language Recognition) combines lexer and parser
generation in a single tool:

\begin{example}[ANTLR Lexer Rules]\label{ex:antlr-lexer}
  \begin{verbatim}
  lexer grammar ExprLexer;
  
  IF     : 'if';
  ELSE   : 'else';
  WHILE  : 'while';
  ID     : [a-zA-Z_] [a-zA-Z0-9_]*;
  NUMBER : [0-9]+;
  WS     : [ \t\n\r]+ -> skip;
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Comparison of Lexer Generators}\label{sec:lexer-comparison}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Lexer Generator Comparison}
  \label{tab:lexer-comparison}
  \begin{tabular}{llll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Tool} & \textbf{Output} & \textbf{Algorithm} & \textbf{Features} \\
    \hline
    Lex & C & DFA & Basic \\
    Flex & C & DFA, table compression & Fast, POSIX \\
    re2c & C (direct-coded) & DFA & High performance \\
    ANTLR & Java/C\#/Python/etc. & ALL(*) & Combined lexer/parser \\
    Alex & Haskell & DFA & Functional \\
    ocamllex & OCaml & DFA & Integrated with Menhir \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

%% ============================================================
%% 16.5 Unicode and Internationalization
%% ============================================================
\section{Unicode and Internationalization}\label{sec:unicode}

Modern programming languages must support Unicode identifiers, string
literals, and source files.  This introduces significant complexity into
lexical analysis.

\subsection{Unicode in Identifiers}\label{sec:unicode-identifiers}

\begin{definition}[Unicode Identifier]\label{def:unicode-identifier}
  A \emph{Unicode identifier} is an identifier that may contain characters
  from any Unicode script, subject to the rules of Unicode Standard Annex
  \#31 (UAX~\#31).
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Unicode Identifiers]\label{ex:unicode-ids}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Python 3: \texttt{名前} (Japanese), \texttt{π值} (Chinese), \texttt{объем} (Russian).
    \item Java: Supports Unicode identifiers since Java~1.1.
    \item Rust: Supports Unicode identifiers with \texttt{XID\_Start} and
      \texttt{XID\_Continue} categories.
    \item Swift: Full Unicode support for identifiers.
  \end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{Unicode Normalization}\label{sec:unicode-normalization}

Unicode allows the same character to be represented in multiple ways (e.g.,
é can be a single codepoint or e + combining accent).  Lexers must handle
this:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{NFC} (Normalization Form C): Composed form.  Most common.
  \item \textbf{NFD} (Normalization Form D): Decomposed form.
  \item \textbf{NFKC/NFKD}: Compatibility forms (normalize confusables).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Source File Encoding}\label{sec:source-encoding}

Source files can be encoded in various character encodings:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{UTF-8}: The dominant encoding for source files.
    ASCII-compatible, variable-width (1--4 bytes per character).
  \item \textbf{UTF-16}: Used by Java internally.  Variable-width (2 or 4
    bytes).
  \item \textbf{UTF-32}: Fixed-width (4 bytes per character).  Rarely used
    for source files.
  \item \textbf{BOM} (Byte Order Mark): A prefix that indicates the
    encoding.  UTF-8 BOM is \texttt{EF BB BF}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Implementation Challenges}\label{sec:unicode-challenges}

Unicode support creates challenges for lexer implementation:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Variable-width encoding}: UTF-8 characters are 1--4 bytes;
    the DFA must operate on bytes, not characters.
  \item \textbf{Character classes}: Unicode property classes (e.g.,
    \texttt{Letter}, \texttt{Number}) are large and complex.
  \item \textbf{Confusable characters}: Some Unicode characters look like
    ASCII but are different (e.g., Cyrillic \texttt{а} vs.\ Latin
    \texttt{a}).
  \item \textbf{Performance}: Unicode-aware lexers are slower than
    ASCII-only lexers.
\end{enumerate}

%% ============================================================
%% 16.6 Whitespace-Sensitive Languages
%% ============================================================
\section{Whitespace-Sensitive Languages}\label{sec:whitespace-sensitive}

Most languages treat whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines) as insignificant,
using it only to separate tokens.  But some languages use indentation as a
syntactic element.

\subsection{Off-Side Rule Languages}\label{sec:off-side-rule}

\begin{definition}[Off-Side Rule]\label{def:off-side-rule}
  The \emph{off-side rule} (named after the football rule by Peter Landin)
  uses indentation to delimit blocks.  A block begins at the indentation
  level of its first statement and ends when the indentation decreases.
\end{definition}

Languages using the off-side rule:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Python}: The most well-known indentation-sensitive language.
  \item \textbf{Haskell}: Uses layout (with optional explicit braces).
  \item \textbf{F\#}: Uses indentation for scoping.
  \item \textbf{CoffeeScript}: Inspired by Python's indentation.
  \item \textbf{Miranda}: The original layout language.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Implementing Indentation-Sensitive Lexing}\label{sec:indent-lexing}

The lexer for an indentation-sensitive language must track indentation
levels and emit synthetic tokens:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
  \caption{Indentation-Sensitive Lexer}
  \label{alg:indent-lexer}
  \begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \State Initialize stack with $[0]$
    \For{each line}
      \State $indent \gets$ number of leading spaces
      \If{$indent > \texttt{stack.top()}$}
        \State Push $indent$ onto stack
        \State Emit \texttt{INDENT} token
      \ElsIf{$indent < \texttt{stack.top()}$}
        \While{$indent < \texttt{stack.top()}$}
          \State Pop stack
          \State Emit \texttt{DEDENT} token
        \EndWhile
      \EndIf
      \State Process rest of line normally
    \EndFor
    \While{stack has more than one element}
      \State Pop stack
      \State Emit \texttt{DEDENT} token
    \EndWhile
  \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Advantages and Disadvantages}\label{sec:whitespace-pros-cons}

\textbf{Advantages}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Eliminates bracket clutter; cleaner visual appearance.
  \item ``What you see is what you get''---indentation matches structure.
  \item Forces consistent formatting.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Disadvantages}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Mixing tabs and spaces can cause subtle bugs.
  \item Copy-pasting code can break indentation.
  \item Harder to generate code programmatically.
  \item Error messages for indentation errors can be confusing.
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 16.7 Implementation Techniques
%% ============================================================
\section{Implementation Techniques}\label{sec:lexical-implementation}

\subsection{Table-Driven Lexers}\label{sec:table-driven}

A table-driven lexer represents the DFA as a transition table:

\begin{verbatim}
  state  | 'a'-'z' | '0'-'9' | other
  -------+---------+---------+------
  q0     | q1      | ERROR   | ERROR
  q1     | q1      | q1      | ACCEPT(ID)
\end{verbatim}

The lexer loop is:
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $s \gets q_0$
\While{true}
  \State $c \gets$ next character
  \State $s \gets \texttt{table}[s][c]$
  \If{$s$ is accepting}
    \State Return token
  \ElsIf{$s$ is error}
    \State Report error
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}

\subsection{Direct-Coded Lexers}\label{sec:direct-coded}

A direct-coded lexer translates the DFA directly into code (nested
switches or gotos), avoiding the overhead of table lookup:

\begin{verbatim}
  state_q0:
    c = next_char()
    if is_alpha(c): goto state_q1
    else: goto error
  
  state_q1:
    c = next_char()
    if is_alpha(c) || is_digit(c): goto state_q1
    else: goto accept_id
\end{verbatim}

Direct-coded lexers are faster but less portable than table-driven lexers.

\subsection{Buffer Management}\label{sec:buffer-management}

Efficient lexers use buffered input with sentinel characters to minimize
I/O:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Double buffering}: Two buffers are loaded alternately; the
    lexer can always look ahead to the end of the buffer.
  \item \textbf{Sentinel characters}: A special character marks the end of
    the buffer, avoiding a bounds check on every character.
  \item \textbf{Read-ahead}: The lexer maintains a pointer to the current
    position and a pointer to the end of the current lexeme.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Lexer--Parser Interaction}\label{sec:lexer-parser-interaction}

The lexer and parser must cooperate:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Token interface}: The lexer provides a \texttt{next\_token()}
    function that the parser calls.
  \item \textbf{Token lookahead}: The parser may need to look ahead $k$
    tokens; the lexer must support this.
  \item \textbf{Error recovery}: When the parser encounters a syntax error,
    the lexer may need to resynchronize.
  \item \textbf{Context sensitivity}: In rare cases (e.g., C typedef
    names), the parser must inform the lexer of the current context.
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
The lexer--parser boundary is a design decision.  Some systems put more
responsibility in the lexer (e.g., handling nested comments); others put
more in the parser (e.g., handling context-sensitive tokens).  The right
balance depends on the language's complexity and the desired error messages.
\end{insight}

%% ============================================================
%% 16.8 Summary
%% ============================================================
% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Retaining Token Checkpoints across Input Buffers}

Running out of buffered characters is not the same as encountering
end of input. A streaming maximal-munch lexer must request more
input before stopping if the automaton can still scan. Assume an
ASCII stream, a deterministic token automaton with a partial
transition function, and no accepting start state. If several rules
accept in one state, its token kind already encodes their priority.
\textsc{Peek} returns the byte at the cursor without consuming it;
it refills as needed and returns EOF only for real end of input.
The retained window permits seeking back to the last accepting
cursor even if the lexer has crossed one or more chunk boundaries.

\begin{sudocode}[Scan one token from a retained window]
\Function{Token}{$p$}
  \If{$\Call{Peek}{p}=\mathrm{EOF}$}
    \State \Return end of stream
  \EndIf
  \State $i\gets p$; $q\gets q_0$; $B\gets\mathrm{none}$
  \Loop
    \State $c\gets\Call{Peek}{i}$ \Comment{Refill, not a token break}
    \If{$c=\mathrm{EOF}$ or $\delta(q,c)$ is missing}
      \State \textbf{break}
    \EndIf
    \State $q\gets\delta(q,c)$; $i\gets i+1$
    \If{$q$ accepts}
      \State $B\gets(i,\mathrm{kind}(q))$ \Comment{Longest so far}
    \EndIf
  \EndLoop
  \If{$B=\mathrm{none}$}
    \State \Return error span $[p,p+1)$
  \EndIf
  \State Read $B$ as $(j,k)$
  \State \Return token $(k,[p,j))$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
Use tokens \texttt{=}, \texttt{==}, and \texttt{x} with chunks
\texttt{=} and \texttt{=x}. Starting at position $0$, the first
byte saves checkpoint $(1,\text{assign})$. Peeking at position
$1$ refills and sees another equals sign. The lexer advances and
saves $(2,\text{equal})$. At $2$ the automaton cannot extend
that token with \texttt{x}; it returns \texttt{==} with span
$[0,2)$. The caller starts the next scan at the returned end
and obtains \texttt{x} on $[2,3)$. If the first chunk had
been the entire stream, the first checkpoint would have won.

\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
$q$ represents $[p,i)$, while $B$ represents the latest
accepting prefix among the bytes scanned. The caller resumes at
the end of the returned span, including after an error, so a bad
byte cannot stall recovery. Keep speculative bytes available until
the next scan consumes them. Arbitrary token DFAs can require
unbounded lookahead and rescanning; a fixed-size retained buffer is
not safe unless the lexer has a suitable bound or another strategy.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:lexical-summary}

Lexical analysis is the first phase of compilation, transforming a character
stream into a token stream.  The theory of regular expressions and finite
automata provides the mathematical foundation, and lexer generators automate
the construction of efficient lexers.

Modern lexical analysis must handle Unicode, whitespace sensitivity, and
interaction with the parser.  Tools like Flex, re2c, and ANTLR provide
different trade-offs between performance, flexibility, and ease of use.

The lexer's design affects the entire compilation pipeline: error messages,
performance, portability, and the parser's complexity.  A well-designed
lexer is essential for a well-designed compiler.
